# Cloudflare Pages response headers. Format: a path pattern, then indented
# `name: value` lines. More specific patterns win over `/*`.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#
# Pages' default for static assets is `public, max-age=14400, must-revalidate`
# — four hours — and Flutter's web build emits `main.dart.js` with NO content
# hash in the filename. `flutter_bootstrap.js` hardcodes `"mainJsPath":
# "main.dart.js"`, and there is no service worker. So nothing anywhere breaks
# the cache: a returning user keeps executing whatever build their browser
# already holds for up to four hours after a deploy.
#
# Measured on the live site before this change (2026-09-24):
#
#   main.dart.js  initiatorType "script"  transferSize 0        encodedBodySize 1345037  <- what the page RAN
#   main.dart.js  initiatorType "fetch"   transferSize 1345885  encodedBodySize 1345585  <- what the network SERVES
#
# Different sizes, and `transferSize: 0` means the browser never asked.
#
# The consequences are not subtle: every web deploy has a rolling four-hour
# window where existing users run the old app; a fix can be "live" and reach
# nobody who already had the site open; two users on the same URL can be on
# different builds; and QA against the deployed app is unreliable by default,
# which caught a session mid-verification with no warning.
#
# WHY REVALIDATION AND NOT A LONGER CACHE WITH HASHED NAMES
#
# A content-hashed filename is the better fix — it makes the bundle immutable
# and cacheable forever. It is not available from the build: `flutter build
# web` has no hashing option in 3.41.7, and the emitted bootstrap names
# `main.dart.js` literally. Hashing would mean renaming the artifact after the
# build and rewriting the bootstrap's buildConfig — machinery this repo would
# own and that would break on each Flutter upgrade. If Flutter gains native
# asset hashing, prefer it and delete these rules.
#
# WHAT REVALIDATION COSTS, measured rather than assumed:
#
#   cold GET   200  4,772,611 bytes  0.265s
#   revalidate 304  0 bytes          0.114s
#
# A 304 carries no body. The cost is one conditional request per asset per
# page load — one round trip, no payload — against a site with a handful of
# users. The cost of the alternative is shipping a fix that reaches nobody
# for four hours.
#
# The rule is blanket on purpose. Nothing this build emits carries a content
# hash, so there is no file for which a long cache is safe. An exception list
# would be a list of things that happen not to have changed yet.
/*
  cache-control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
